---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

// Support page for the Net-DNS-Monitor Mac App Store edition. App Store Connect
// requires a support URL with real contact information; this is it. Facts about
// the store edition come from that app's docs/APP_STORE_SUBMISSION.md.
const title = "Net-DNS-Monitor support";
const description =
  "Help and contact for Net-DNS-Monitor, the macOS menu bar app that watches your network and DNS connectivity and tells you what is actually wrong when it breaks.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} route="/net-dns-monitor/" twin={null}>
  <p class="lede">{description}</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:mitchphudson@gmail.com">mitchphudson@gmail.com</a>. Say which
    version of macOS you are on and which version of the app (the About panel shows it),
    and attach the incident report if the question is about one; the app's menu has
    <strong>Open last report</strong>. Reports stay on your Mac until you choose to
    send one.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app does</h2>
  <p>
    Net-DNS-Monitor sits in the menu bar and checks, every few seconds, whether your Mac
    can reach the internet and resolve names. When a check fails it separates the three
    things that look identical from a browser: your Mac's own link, the local network,
    and DNS. It runs an offline troubleshooting ladder, records what each step found, and
    writes an incident report you can hand to whoever runs your network.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Mac App Store edition cannot do</h2>
  <p>
    Every Mac App Store app runs inside the App Sandbox and may not ask for administrator
    rights. The store edition therefore leaves out the features that need either, and
    says so in its reports rather than failing in a way that looks like a network fault:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>switching your network service order to a backup connection;</li>
    <li>flushing the DNS cache or renewing a DHCP lease;</li>
    <li>reading the macOS unified log for evidence;</li>
    <li>the built-in router, the shell console, and starting at login on its own.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To start the app at login, add it in System Settings → General → Login Items. Every
    other feature, including the diagnosis, the reports, the graphs and the optional
    alerts, works the same as in the full edition.
  </p>

  <h2>First launch</h2>
  <p>
    macOS asks whether the app may find devices on your local network. Allowing it lets
    the app check whether your router answers and compare notes with other Macs running
    the app nearby. If you decline, those checks report failures; you can change the
    answer later in System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Local Network.
  </p>

  <h2>Claude diagnosis</h2>
  <p>
    The optional diagnosis by Claude uses your own Anthropic API key, entered under
    <strong>Credentials</strong> in the app menu and kept in your Keychain. Nothing is
    sent to Anthropic until you choose <strong>Allow Claude diagnosis…</strong> and
    confirm the dialog that lists exactly what an incident report would contain.
    <strong>Withdraw Claude permission</strong> reverses it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where your data lives</h2>
  <p>
    Settings, incident reports and the connection history stay in the app's own
    container on your Mac. The publisher receives none of it. The
    <a href="/net-dns-monitor/privacy/">privacy policy</a> lists everything that can
    leave your Mac, when, and how to turn each case off.
  </p>
</Base>
